  "notes": "Used for supporting a superior in an official or organizational context. Also functions as a suru verb ({補佐|ほさ}する). As a title (sense 2), it is attached after a position name.\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {補佐|ほさ}する — to assist, to support\n- {補佐|ほさ}{役|やく} — supporting role, assistant\n- {補佐|ほさ}{官|かん} — aide, advisor (government/military title)\n- {大統領|だいとうりょう}{補佐|ほさ}{官|かん} — presidential advisor\n- {課長|かちょう}{補佐|ほさ} — assistant section chief\n\nUSAGE:\nAs a title, {補佐|ほさ} comes after the position it supports: {部長|ぶちょう}{補佐|ほさ} (assistant department head). This is a common rank in Japanese government and corporate hierarchies.\n\nSIMILAR WORDS:\n- {助手|じょしゅ} — assistant (general, often academic)\n- {秘書|ひしょ} — secretary (personal support role)\n- {副|ふく} — vice-, deputy- (prefix for deputy positions)",
